106
00:17:50.030 --> 00:17:56.159
Alex Popowycz (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): through block node discoverability, find another block node to send it to, and there is some scalability there that, like.

107
00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:56.850
Richard Bair: That's correct.

108
00:17:56.850 --> 00:18:02.789
Alex Popowycz (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): live with 4 to 1, whereas if you lose enough consensus nodes, the network stops.

109
00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:11.330
Richard Bair: also true. However, if all the block nodes stop getting rewarded because they were deprioritized under consensus nodes, then they might all just turn themselves off and stop the network.

110
00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:25.020
Alex Popowycz (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): Right, right. No, no, agreed, agreed. So, it was just, the consideration of looking at, was there any inherent or capability or mechanism to address that, particularly if

111
00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:33.349
Alex Popowycz (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): We have a 1 to 1 block node to a consensus node ratio when we could easily survive with a, say, 4 to 1.

112
00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:42.860
Richard Bair: No, I don't think so. I think the simplest thing for us to do is to just… and there's also a benefit to it. Let me…

113
00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:57.669
Richard Bair: So the simplest thing for us to do is to just bundle the two rewards together. We don't have to have a separate account for a different operator, we don't have to have, you know, separate, you know, reward, logic and so forth. All we do is just say.

114
00:18:57.770 --> 00:19:06.409
Richard Bair: If you're a consensus node, and you're running a block node, then we increase the amount of reward you get by some, you know, by some amount.

115
00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:22.079
Richard Bair: And that'll actually encourage people… in fact, the fact that you pay out of the same pool should encourage consensus node operators to want to run block nodes, because, you know, if you are going to, sort of run down

116
00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:41.019
Richard Bair: the amount of node rewards, which, just to be clear, should never happen on… on the system. Like, we should… we should… I mean, in the short term, it happens, but, but… but shouldn't. Like, I think Hedera is funding that account until fees are sufficient to keep it rolling.

117
00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:55.749
Richard Bair: But, you know, you're gonna get a bigger slice of what's left, let's say, if you're running a block node than if you're not. So, I guess it could be argued that running a… you know, getting them out of the same pool is actually an incentive to people to run block nodes, which is kind of what we want anyway.

118
00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:01.060
Alex Popowycz (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): Okay.

119
00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:08.879
