00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:55.620
Michael Kantor: Gotcha, thanks.

127
00:20:55.620 --> 00:21:02.840
Alex Popowycz (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): And just to confirm that when we say Tier 1 block node, that also indicates that that's also a full history?

128
00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:05.160
Alex Popowycz (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): Block node.

129
00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:16.710
Richard Bair: Yeah, I mean, should be. So, this is one of the little details that is worth discussing or making sure everybody's aware of, because it really drives a lot of the design.

130
00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:23.119
Richard Bair: There is no known way to verify that a block node is honest.

131
00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:41.729
Richard Bair: Now, fundamentally, block nodes don't have to be honest, because the blocks themselves are self-contained, cryptographically, you know, have everything that they need. You can get that block from an honest person, a dishonest person, or a carrier pigeon, it doesn't make any difference. When you take that block, you can independently verify that the block is legit and real.

132
00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:47.569
Richard Bair: And vice versa, you can independently verify that a block is fake. So you don't have to, like, inherently trust anybody.

133
00:21:47.780 --> 00:22:02.279
Richard Bair: But, so the only thing that a block node can do for you is it could just, throw away your blocks, so it could acknowledge to the consensus node, saying, hey, thank you for your block, you're a wonderful, you know, I'm… I'm a great block node, I'm here to serve you, and I'll just throw this thing away.

134
00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:13.190
Richard Bair: Or it could fail to forward blocks to other users, or respond with blocks when users ask for them. So a user says, hey, give me block 100, and it goes, but I don't have block 100 when it does.

135
00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:26.250
Richard Bair: So there… those are the failure modes that can… or can delay responses, like, instead of streaming it directly to you, it sort of buffers it up for a while before it sends it to you. Those are the sort of things that a block node can do to you.

136
00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:36.919
Richard Bair: Now, you would like to be able to have a reward system for your block nodes, where any honest block node gets rewarded. The trick is trying to figure out, how do you know that you have an honest block node?

137
00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:48.769
Richard Bair: Because you could, I could… let's say that I have, 100 block nodes, and I'm getting rewards for all of them. If you ask any of them for a block, they return the block.

138
00:22:48.860 --> 00:23:08.309
Richard Bair: But actually, all 100 of those block nodes doesn't store a single block. All it does is go ask another block node for a block. If you ask me for one, then I'll go ask somebody else for one. And I'm getting, like, 100 times the rewards for returning a block that I didn't actually do any work to maintain, and I didn't actually make this better for the network.

139
00:23:09.170 --> 00:23:27.440
Richard Bair: This is also true, so for the consensus nodes, the essential thing for the consensus nodes is they want to know that the blocks that they've produced have been saved by at least one honest block node in the world. Somewhere out there, that block exists. Otherwise, it'll be gone forever.

140
00:23:27.440 --> 00:23:41.070
Richard Bair: So if consensus nodes were to sort of pick block nodes at random from a pool of all possible block nodes and say, I'm going to send my blocks to that block node, it's possible that all the consensus nodes will select dishonest block nodes

141
00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:56.629
Richard Bair: Yeah, because block nodes are cheap to set up. You can do a Sybil attack, basically. Set up a million block nodes, and then all of a sudden, you know, consensus nodes are submitting them, and those dishonest block nodes are pretending that they're saving those blocks that were given, but they actually throw them away.

142
00:23:56.660 --> 00:24:08.630
Richard Bair: Or maybe they just have one person holding onto it for as long as they run the attack, and then when the attack is over, that one person throws them away. Right? There's ways for them to just, like, chuck these things without passing them on to anybody else.

143
00:24:08.630 --> 00:24:16.739
Richard Bair: And the consensus nodes would just be happily creating new blocks and handing it off, and then the consensus nodes, you know, it turns out that our blockchain history is lost.

144
00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:27.999
Richard Bair: That could happen if you just pick at random. Now, we could try to prevent that by requiring every block node to bond. So if we did that, then every block node that comes up has to pay, you know.

145
00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:39.200
Richard Bair: $10,000 to be a block note or something and get listed in the registry, then the consensus nodes could pick at random, and then they could start sending blocks to those. Okay, that's great. We've got the bonding part, so at least it's difficult to set up a million of these.

146
00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:54.269
Richard Bair: So the probability that you'll get, you know, all 39 nodes will pick dishonest block nodes is probably not that high, but, you know, I mean, it's still there. It just depends on the ratio of honest to dishonest block nodes in the… in the registry, and if there were only, say.

147
00:24:54.270 --> 00:24:59.689
Richard Bair: you know, a dozen honest block nodes, then an attacker only probably needs a, you know.

148
00:24:59.930 --> 00:25:09.309
Richard Bair: a couple hundred of these, and they'd be able to operate the attack. Still fairly expensive attack to pull off if your bond is high enough, so that might be okay.

149
00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:23.420
Richard Bair: But you're sort of depending on that bond to save you, because we don't have any method for slashing. There is no way for us to deterministically know whether a block node is a good guy or a bad guy. It's just no way to know deterministically.

150
00:25:23.690 --> 00:25:29.830
Richard Bair: So, so at least for now, the strategy… so rather than trying to…

151
00:25:30.110 --> 00:25:45.520
Richard Bair: you know, hold up this hip until we have a really great strategy worked out for dealing with this attack model. The thought was every council, or sorry, every consensus node

152
00:25:46.300 --> 00:25:50.090
Richard Bair: Needs to select block nodes that it trusts.

153
00:25:51.240 --> 00:26:02.569
Richard Bair: And, so there's sort of an inherent trust relationship between the Tier 1 block node and the consensus node. And that is the only thing that makes a Tier 1 block node a Tier 1 block node.

154
00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:04.660
Richard Bair: So there is a trust.

155
00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:09.419
Richard Bair: that all those Tier 1 block nodes are going to be storing all of the history forever.

156
00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:13.890
Richard Bair: well, we can't enforce it, but that's the… that's the assumption. Now, since…

157
00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:30.169
Richard Bair: the block… the Tier 1, you know, since the consensus nodes are all operated today by council members, and therefore all Tier 1 block nodes will be ones that are operated by Council members, yeah, we'll say that they're all full history.

158
00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:34.530
Richard Bair: So that gives us that level of safety and redundancy.

159
00:26:34.650 --> 00:26:50.780
Richard Bair: But, you know, one day when council members are not the only ones operating consensus nodes, then, you know, there's… it'll still be the case that we will require whoever is a consensus node has to select a set of block nodes that they trust.

160
00:26:50.880 --> 00:27:08.099
Richard Bair: And that works out because, our trust model on the network is always that there are, you know, less than one-third dishonest nodes, or there's more than two-thirds honest nodes. So even in a future where arbitrary nodes are being used as consensus nodes, it would be

161
00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:16.399
Richard Bair: You know, more than two-thirds have to be honest. And if they have picked block nodes that they explicitly trust, not sort of algorithmically picked up.

162
00:27:16.430 --> 00:27:24.269
Richard Bair: then it's still safe. And part of that trust is the assumption that the ones that they're trusting are saving all of the block data.

163
00:27:24.550 --> 00:27:37.820
Richard Bair: Maybe one day in the future, we'll, you know, have another solution, or maybe we'll just, you know, say, if you want your block node to be a Tier 1 block node, you have to bond an enormous amount of money just to make it really difficult to get Sybil attacks. And then,

164
00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:44.969
Richard Bair: You know, and then we just go ahead and do a deterministic selection, or, you know, deterministic random selection, maybe that will work out.

165
00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:49.859
Richard Bair: But anyway, that's sort of the core problem space. So the assumption that we have here is…

